<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Map of Teozacoalco</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1580">1580</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Map of Teozacoalco is a painted map of 1580 from the Mixtec town of Teozacoalco in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, made for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/relaciones-geograficas">Relaciones Geográficas</a> ordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a>. It combines a map of the town's lands with genealogies of its rulers. In 1949 the archaeologist Alfonso Caso used it to show that the Mixtec codices record real dynasties and places.</p></div>
